From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Harris <wfh@chromium.org>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 08:19:36 -0700
Subject: Limit peer trust for isolated connections

NodeLink::OnAcceptIntroduction now rejects introductions that would
introduce a broker to another broker; broker-to-broker links are only
ever established directly via ConnectNode, and HandleIntroductionRequest
already declines to introduce two brokers, so this case is never reached
by a well-behaved sender.

NodeLink::OnRelayMessage now also requires the sender to be a
non-broker, matching the only legitimate caller (NodeLink::Transmit via
GetBrokerLink()).

In the mojo ipcz driver, Transport::Deserialize no longer treats the
source as trusted purely because the destination is a broker when the
source is itself a broker: a non-broker still implicitly trusts its
broker, but two brokers connected to one another must explicitly mark
the peer as trusted before either will accept an embedded transport that
claims a broker destination or a trusted peer. The mojo-ipcz test driver
is updated so cooperating broker test nodes mark each other as trusted,
and a new TransportFromUntrustedBroker test covers the untrusted-broker
case directly.

